id: pm-5z9plz
title: "A measurement ratchet bound stops enforcing the moment its owner reaches a terminal status, so every guarded population goes unbounded exactly when its fix ships"
description: "The tracker measurement ratchet enforces a bound only while the item that owns it is open. The evaluator computes retired as TERMINAL_OWNER_STATUSES.includes(ownerStatus) with TERMINAL_OWNER_STATUSES frozen to closed and canceled, and a retired declaration short-circuits to ok true with reason retired_with_owner before any comparison runs. The header states the rationale directly: bounds retire themselves so that no bound outlives the property it guards, and honest work never needs a waiver to close.\n\nThat rationale contains a category error. The property a declaration guards is a population count over the record, such as the number of dependency rows of kind blocks. That population exists for as long as the graph exists. What terminates when the owning item closes is the remediation work, not the property. Retirement is therefore keyed to the lifetime of the fix and applied to the lifetime of the measurement, and the two are not the same interval. A ratchet exists precisely to hold ground after a fix ships; this one releases the ground at that exact moment.\n\nMeasured live on 2026-08-09 at commit 9a0a048b6, with the gate reporting twenty-three enforced and three retired declarations. Every one of the twenty-three enforced declarations sits at observed equal to bound exactly. The only declarations whose observed value has drifted away from its declaration are the retired ones. dependency-kind-blocks declares a ceiling of 241 measured on 2026-08-04 and observes 243. dependency-kind-related-to declares a ceiling of 43 and observes 50. Both report ok true with reason retired_with_owner. Both owners, pm-jkbqt8 and pm-4020c5, reached closed at 2026-08-08 around 23:25Z, and the rows that carried each population past its ceiling were written earlier the same evening, between 21:55Z and 22:50Z, by ordinary maintenance sessions using the supported surface. The breach therefore existed while the bounds were still enforced and was never reported, because the next evaluation of the gate ran after the owners had closed and the declarations had retired.\n\nNeither individual retirement is wrong, and this item does not propose reversing either. Both fixes are real and were verified in a sandbox at this commit. Writing a dependency of kind related_to now canonicalizes to related on write, so that population is frozen legacy residue. A reverse blocks row now correctly classifies its dependent as blocked under pm next, so blocks rows are no longer invisible to the readiness resolver. The defect is that the vocabulary offers no way to record the state those two correct decisions should have produced. A declaration is either enforced against a stale number or retired into no enforcement at all. There is no third state meaning the fix shipped, the population is correct at its current value, and it may not grow again. That third state is what a ratchet is for, and it is unexpressible.\n\nThree consequences follow, all observable in the current document.\n\nThe guarded population becomes unbounded and unmeasured. The count of blocks rows is 243 today with nothing enforcing it. blocks remains a writable dependency kind, so if the corpus-aware reverse resolution regresses, or a maintenance pass adds ordering rows in the invisible direction again, no declaration counts them.\n\nThe declared number freezes and silently misrepresents itself. The re-baseline path in buildUpdatedDeclarations returns the declaration untouched when entry.retired is true, so the update flag can never refresh a retired observation. The document therefore carries 241 with measured_on 2026-08-04 next to declarations that are current, with nothing in the file marking it as neither current nor enforced.\n\nCancellation silences a guard exactly as closure does. canceled is a member of TERMINAL_OWNER_STATUSES, so abandoning remediation retires the bound on identical terms to completing it. The gate's own failure guidance names this as the remedy, telling a reader that re-declaring is not the remedy and to move the owning item to a terminal status to retire the bound. The cheapest such move is cancellation. A failing ratchet can therefore be silenced by one status mutation, the gate then reports ok true, and nothing in the report distinguishes a guard dropped because the hazard is gone from a guard dropped because the work stopped.\n\nThe correct polarity already exists in the same directory and is not shared. The surface replication gate honours a waiver only while it names a pm item and carries an expires_on date that is greater than or equal to today, so a suppression there decays and enforcement resumes automatically. The measurement ratchet inverts this: the bound decays and enforcement ends permanently. Two gates in one directory, opposite lifetime polarities, with nothing reconciling them.\n\nThe remedy is a declared disposition for what happens when an owner reaches a terminal status, defaulting to continued enforcement rather than silence, with explicit retirement requiring a recorded reason, and with cancellation never retiring a bound implicitly."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[5]: "area:release",ci,governance,measurement,ratchet
